00:02:44.190 --> 00:02:45.670
your partners.

00:02:45.670 --> 00:02:48.820
So let's explore
kind of why that

00:02:48.820 --> 00:02:51.580
is to give you a
fundamental knowledge of why

00:02:51.580 --> 00:02:53.740
this technology is
going to change computer

00:02:53.740 --> 00:02:56.820
architecture as we know it.

00:02:56.820 --> 00:02:58.910
So here's kind of
the classical pyramid

00:02:58.910 --> 00:03:00.830
diagram of current
technologies when

00:03:00.830 --> 00:03:02.940
it comes to memory and storage.

00:03:02.940 --> 00:03:05.210
Now you may notice there
are big gaps between these,

00:03:05.210 --> 00:03:07.002
and in the realm of
computer architectures,

00:03:07.002 --> 00:03:08.820
these are oceans away.

00:03:08.820 --> 00:03:11.630
So at the very top, we have
memory, which currently

00:03:11.630 --> 00:03:14.040
the mainstay is DRAM memory.

00:03:14.040 --> 00:03:17.780
So this is tiny capacities
compared to what you

00:03:17.780 --> 00:03:20.120
can see in solid state devices.

00:03:20.120 --> 00:03:23.340
It can be relatively expensive
but it's really fast.

00:03:23.340 --> 00:03:27.420
We actually measure latency in
nanoseconds within this realm.

00:03:27.420 --> 00:03:29.990
Now we cross the gap and
we enter the storage realm,

00:03:29.990 --> 00:03:33.470
which currently the two
main technologies are

00:03:33.470 --> 00:03:37.820
flash NAND SSDs and then as
well as spinning magnetic disks,

00:03:37.820 --> 00:03:40.310
like hard drives, or even tape.

00:03:40.310 --> 00:03:41.990
Typically, latency
measured in these

00:03:41.990 --> 00:03:44.010
are within the realm
of microseconds.

00:03:44.010 --> 00:03:47.300
So again, a magnitude different
than what we have with memory.

00:03:47.300 --> 00:03:50.510
Though in the storage realm,
they have higher capacities,

00:03:50.510 --> 00:03:56.030
they're fairly affordable
for the gigabyte per cost,

00:03:56.030 --> 00:03:59.940
but again, they're a magnitude
different in performance.

00:03:59.940 --> 00:04:04.850
So this bottleneck has actually
been in computer architecture

00:04:04.850 --> 00:04:07.340
for quite some time, where
actually software has really

00:04:07.340 --> 00:04:10.370
just worked around this
by really optimizing code

00:04:10.370 --> 00:04:13.250
to make an advantage of it.

00:04:13.250 --> 00:04:15.620
Now how we're
changing this equation

00:04:15.620 --> 00:04:19.010
is by this new technology called
Intel Optane DC Persistent

00:04:19.010 --> 00:04:19.820
Memory--

00:04:19.820 --> 00:04:23.420
really the first mainstream
persistent memory technology

00:04:23.420 --> 00:04:25.890
that's coming to
the marketplace.

00:04:25.890 --> 00:04:28.970
So this is big, affordable,
as well as the data on it

00:04:28.970 --> 00:04:32.780
can be persistent even after a
server reboot, a power loss, et

00:04:32.780 --> 00:04:33.800
cetera.

00:04:33.800 --> 00:04:35.820
We're coming to
market with the 128,

00:04:35.820 --> 00:04:37.820
256, and 512, and
actually in my pocket,

00:04:37.820 --> 00:04:42.810
I have one of our 512
capacities right here.

00:04:42.810 --> 00:04:45.920
So with these, you can
have a total of six

00:04:45.920 --> 00:04:50.290
of these on a second generation
Xeon processor platform

00:04:50.290 --> 00:04:52.730
and you pair those with a DRAM.

00:04:52.730 --> 00:04:54.410
So on one socket, six of these.

00:04:54.410 --> 00:04:57.510
On a standard two-socket
server, you can have up to 12.

00:04:57.510 --> 00:05:00.350
So if you take the 512,
multiply that by 12,

00:05:00.350 --> 00:05:03.680
you have a total of six
terabytes of persistent memory

00:05:03.680 --> 00:05:07.000
available on your platform.

00:05:07.000 --> 00:05:10.090
Now also, I will mention that
since now in the memory realm,

00:05:10.090 --> 00:05:12.980
data's at rest, security
is quite important.

00:05:12.980 --> 00:05:17.640
So these actually come in with
standard hardware encryption.

00:05:17.640 --> 00:05:20.950
All right, now I'm going to give
just a quick download of how we

00:05:20.950 --> 00:05:23.030
actually use this technology.

00:05:23.030 --> 00:05:26.240
So there are specifically
two operating modes.

00:05:26.240 --> 00:05:28.990
So think of one as
being persistent memory,

00:05:28.990 --> 00:05:31.750
and I'll get into details
of how you can use that,

00:05:31.750 --> 00:05:34.000
and the other one is
actually kind of our easy way

00:05:34.000 --> 00:05:36.460
to take advantage of that
large memory footprint

00:05:36.460 --> 00:05:39.610
but without the persistency,
and we call that memory mode.

00:05:39.610 --> 00:05:41.717
So let me talk about
app direct mode.

00:05:41.717 --> 00:05:43.300
So in app direct
mode, this technology

00:05:43.300 --> 00:05:46.070
is transparent to your
operating system, your users,

00:05:46.070 --> 00:05:49.300
your application,
and smart ISVs--

00:05:49.300 --> 00:05:51.370
Independent Software
Vendors-- like SAP,

00:05:51.370 --> 00:05:53.763
who understand their data
structures very well,

00:05:53.763 --> 00:05:55.180
will put the data
structures which

00:05:55.180 --> 00:05:56.620
make the most
sense in the medium

00:05:56.620 --> 00:05:57.920
that they have available.

00:05:57.920 --> 00:05:59.500
So for those data
structures that

00:05:59.500 --> 00:06:01.420
make the most
sense in DRAM where

00:06:01.420 --> 00:06:04.360
you need that high bandwidth,
they can put the data structure

00:06:04.360 --> 00:06:06.940
there, but if you
could utilize maybe

00:06:06.940 --> 00:06:09.850
a lot of persistent
memory and take advantage

00:06:09.850 --> 00:06:12.340
of it being persistent
if I had to do a server

00:06:12.340 --> 00:06:14.530
patch or something, it's
able to come up quickly

00:06:14.530 --> 00:06:16.990
versus previous generations.

00:06:16.990 --> 00:06:20.832
I will make note, with app
direct, there is that usage

00:06:20.832 --> 00:06:23.290
but there is actually a second
usage where you can actually

00:06:23.290 --> 00:06:25.600
use standard storage APIs.

00:06:25.600 --> 00:06:28.310
So instead, if you wanted to
just use standard block IO

00:06:28.310 --> 00:06:33.370
or actually boot from the DIMMs
itself, we do support that.

00:06:33.370 --> 00:06:35.590
Now with memory mode,
how this is utilized

00:06:35.590 --> 00:06:37.540
is basically the
platform is going

00:06:37.540 --> 00:06:39.250
to consume the
DRAM in your system

00:06:39.250 --> 00:06:42.130
to act as a really
fast write-back cache.

00:06:42.130 --> 00:06:43.840
So from an application
standpoint,

00:06:43.840 --> 00:06:46.780
that six terabytes I mentioned
before, we'll just see that six

00:06:46.780 --> 00:06:50.770
terabyte and won't see the DRAM
behind that acting as a cache.

00:06:50.770 --> 00:06:53.080
So with, this, again
this is a volatile usage,

00:06:53.080 --> 00:06:55.480
and really this is to be
out-of-the-box if you want

00:06:55.480 --> 00:06:58.660
to have six terabytes
available of volatile memory.

00:06:58.660 --> 00:07:00.720
This is the route to go.

00:07:00.720 --> 00:07:03.950
Now hopefully that's a quick 101
of Intel Optane DC Persistent

00:07:03.950 --> 00:07:04.450
Memory.

