00:10:40.000 --> 00:10:42.790
So when I think of data
management challenges,

00:10:42.790 --> 00:10:45.170
obviously we know our
data always grows.

00:10:45.170 --> 00:10:49.160
There's always a consistent
cost of how do we manage it?

00:10:49.160 --> 00:10:51.280
What's our operational budget?

00:10:51.280 --> 00:10:55.330
And then how can we get answers
and solve problems quicker

00:10:55.330 --> 00:10:56.110
for our customers?

00:10:56.110 --> 00:10:58.580
So decrease time to value.

00:10:58.580 --> 00:11:00.400
When I think of
persistent memory,

00:11:00.400 --> 00:11:03.590
I think it solves kind
of this heat map issue.

00:11:03.590 --> 00:11:07.430
So traditionally-- and Jack
referred to this previously--

00:11:07.430 --> 00:11:08.990
you just had three tiers.

00:11:08.990 --> 00:11:11.930
You had your DRAM
for your in-memory.

00:11:11.930 --> 00:11:13.380
And then for things--

00:11:13.380 --> 00:11:16.010
now I have to admit, in
another previous life

00:11:16.010 --> 00:11:18.230
I used to be a DBA.

00:11:18.230 --> 00:11:20.320
And even within memory
you don't store everything

00:11:20.320 --> 00:11:21.700
in the in-memory database.

00:11:21.700 --> 00:11:26.440
So like your data that's your
quarterly or monthly reports,

00:11:26.440 --> 00:11:29.140
you likely will put
that in warm data, which

00:11:29.140 --> 00:11:31.180
is the orange represented here.

00:11:31.180 --> 00:11:34.750
And the blue data is
for your big data stuff

00:11:34.750 --> 00:11:39.650
that you'd store in cloudera
or something similar.

00:11:39.650 --> 00:11:42.700
Now with persistent memory
and this larger capacity

00:11:42.700 --> 00:11:47.150
what you see is kind of
a emerging or a new zone,

00:11:47.150 --> 00:11:49.670
the second to the left there.

00:11:49.670 --> 00:11:51.620
The Intel Optane DC
Persistent Memory zone,

00:11:51.620 --> 00:11:59.550
which has both the capacity to
store warm and and hot data.

00:12:03.180 --> 00:12:05.180
Now when I think
about this, what do I

00:12:05.180 --> 00:12:06.800
I think about at
a database layer?

00:12:06.800 --> 00:12:11.300
Specifically the main memory,
or about 95% of your data,

00:12:11.300 --> 00:12:14.120
can now be stored in
persistent memory.

00:12:14.120 --> 00:12:17.180
This also applies to
potential new features

00:12:17.180 --> 00:12:21.870
that were added in HANA
2.3, like extension nodes.

00:12:21.870 --> 00:12:24.470
So that gives you an opportunity
to put persistent memory

00:12:24.470 --> 00:12:29.480
both in the hot and
the warm data tiers.

00:12:29.480 --> 00:12:31.420
Now what I also think
about persistent memory,

00:12:31.420 --> 00:12:38.650
it's we are driving the cost
down of compute on HANA.

00:12:38.650 --> 00:12:42.510
When I think of historical
on-premise scenario,

00:12:42.510 --> 00:12:47.040
I'm hearing customers just last
year were buying new systems.

00:12:47.040 --> 00:12:49.410
And they're telling
me that about 60%

00:12:49.410 --> 00:12:53.783
of the cost of that system
was just the memory card.

00:12:53.783 --> 00:12:55.950
So we're going to drive
down that cost with the dems

00:12:55.950 --> 00:12:57.505
that we just released.

00:12:57.505 --> 00:12:59.880
And there's going to be better
opportunities for business

00:12:59.880 --> 00:13:00.480
continuity.

00:13:00.480 --> 00:13:03.060
And this is a good place
where GCP plays it.

00:13:03.060 --> 00:13:04.860
I'm hearing some
customers that kind of

00:13:04.860 --> 00:13:09.845
have a hybrid scenario where
they will still host locally

00:13:09.845 --> 00:13:11.220
on their data
center, but they're

00:13:11.220 --> 00:13:14.277
going to use replication
to do disaster

00:13:14.277 --> 00:13:16.110
recovery, for instance,
to the Google Cloud.

00:13:16.110 --> 00:13:19.477
So that's something to think
about in your scenario.

00:13:19.477 --> 00:13:21.810
And I already talked a little
bit about increased memory

00:13:21.810 --> 00:13:23.730
capacity.

00:13:23.730 --> 00:13:24.230
OK.

00:13:24.230 --> 00:13:27.900
So when we delve a
little deeper we're

00:13:27.900 --> 00:13:31.830
also seeing with this increased
capacity an opportunity not

00:13:31.830 --> 00:13:35.670
just from a lower dem
price perspective or price

00:13:35.670 --> 00:13:36.780
per gigabyte.

00:13:36.780 --> 00:13:40.380
There's opportunities to
consolidate multiple instances.

00:13:40.380 --> 00:13:43.050
Intel IT in particular
were actually

00:13:43.050 --> 00:13:51.130
in the process of consolidating
both our ECC and another HANA

00:13:51.130 --> 00:13:53.200
native instance into one.

00:13:53.200 --> 00:13:56.410
So look for solutions
where you're

00:13:56.410 --> 00:14:00.167
discussing that in detail here
in the next couple of weeks

00:14:00.167 --> 00:14:01.125
leading up to Sapphire.

00:14:04.340 --> 00:14:05.960
The other thing I
like to think about,

00:14:05.960 --> 00:14:09.530
and I alluded to this earlier,
I call this the layer cake.

00:14:09.530 --> 00:14:11.540
But as you can see,
this is a piece of cake

00:14:11.540 --> 00:14:13.530
that's fallen on its side.

00:14:13.530 --> 00:14:16.730
And now we have
the area in green

00:14:16.730 --> 00:14:20.580
is where persistent
memory falls into place.

00:14:20.580 --> 00:14:23.000
That is your main data stores.

00:14:23.000 --> 00:14:26.180
Now as Jack said to
previously, you're

00:14:26.180 --> 00:14:30.160
still going to need some
DRAM, but the vast majority

00:14:30.160 --> 00:14:35.020
of the HANA database in orange
is where your main memory

00:14:35.020 --> 00:14:36.110
is going to be stored.

00:14:36.110 --> 00:14:39.250
So it's going to
vary per customer,

00:14:39.250 --> 00:14:42.460
but rough estimates I'm
hearing is between 95%,

00:14:42.460 --> 00:14:46.540
96% of your data will now
be in persistent memory.

00:14:46.540 --> 00:14:49.660
And that's going to
improve your loading,

00:14:49.660 --> 00:14:52.600
reloading of the
database at startup.

00:14:52.600 --> 00:14:55.960
It's going to improve the
time, the data load of that,

00:14:55.960 --> 00:14:56.710
significantly.

00:14:56.710 --> 00:15:01.250
It's going to go from
potentially up to an hour

00:15:01.250 --> 00:15:01.750
to minutes.

00:15:04.350 --> 00:15:07.200
The last thing I want to talk
about is business continuity.

00:15:07.200 --> 00:15:08.700
Now, there's lots
of things when you

00:15:08.700 --> 00:15:10.050
think of business continuity.

00:15:10.050 --> 00:15:13.950
The first thing I think of,
first and foremost, is backup.

00:15:13.950 --> 00:15:17.940
As you can see, this
actually shows an opportunity

00:15:17.940 --> 00:15:21.720
to introduce persistent memory
into your existing environment,

00:15:21.720 --> 00:15:25.500
where you still have some
older systems that just have

00:15:25.500 --> 00:15:29.460
all DRAM, and now you can
do a replication scenario

00:15:29.460 --> 00:15:32.010
where you can use
persistent memory

00:15:32.010 --> 00:15:35.260
on the right with the orange--

00:15:35.260 --> 00:15:39.000
the darker orange-- with
some DRAM because you now

00:15:39.000 --> 00:15:40.570
have a bigger footprint.

00:15:40.570 --> 00:15:43.740
This can also be
used for backup.

00:15:43.740 --> 00:15:45.540
This can be used for
disaster recovery

00:15:45.540 --> 00:15:47.760
at a different location.

00:15:47.760 --> 00:15:53.770
It could be used on the
Google Cloud, as an example.

00:15:53.770 --> 00:15:56.650
And the last one is a new use
case with extension nodes.

00:15:56.650 --> 00:15:58.530
So extension nodes
was a new feature

00:15:58.530 --> 00:16:00.626
that was added in HANA 2.3.

00:16:00.626 --> 00:16:03.630
It does not replace
dynamic tiering.

00:16:03.630 --> 00:16:05.100
That still exists.

00:16:05.100 --> 00:16:11.580
But it's a feature that allows
you to place your warm data

00:16:11.580 --> 00:16:13.770
into persistent
memory, and then has

00:16:13.770 --> 00:16:15.940
the scaling factor of 8 to 1.

00:16:15.940 --> 00:16:20.340
In other words, you can
have eight times as much

00:16:20.340 --> 00:16:26.970
persistent memory in the dark
orange as you have with DRAM.

00:16:26.970 --> 00:16:29.450
So look to SAP to
get more details

00:16:29.450 --> 00:16:31.580
on how to implement that.

00:16:31.580 --> 00:16:34.220
The other thing nice
with extension nodes

00:16:34.220 --> 00:16:38.780
is it gives you a full HANA
instance for your warm data,

00:16:38.780 --> 00:16:43.370
and then that will give you full
access to all the PAL libraries

00:16:43.370 --> 00:16:46.200
to do analytics.

00:16:46.200 --> 00:16:49.650
With that, I'll turn
it over to Andreas.

00:16:49.650 --> 00:16:51.150
ANDREAS SCHUSTER:
Thanks a lot, Tim.

00:16:51.150 --> 00:16:53.670
So microphone is working.

00:16:53.670 --> 00:16:54.960
My name is Andreas Schuster.

00:16:54.960 --> 00:16:57.085
I flew here all the way
from [INAUDIBLE],, Germany,

00:16:57.085 --> 00:17:01.050
so I'm representing SAP in
this long-standing cooperation

00:17:01.050 --> 00:17:04.280
between the three companies.

00:17:04.280 --> 00:17:08.160
I'm the product manager for
persistent memory in SAP HANA.

00:17:08.160 --> 00:17:11.400
And before I get started and
telling us a little bit more

00:17:11.400 --> 00:17:15.150
about how HANA is actually using
it and what it means for HANA,

00:17:15.150 --> 00:17:16.920
let's briefly wrap up
what we just heard.

00:17:16.920 --> 00:17:19.410
So we had Jack in the
very beginning introducing

00:17:19.410 --> 00:17:24.420
the technology itself of Intel
Optane DC persistent memory,

00:17:24.420 --> 00:17:29.760
this new, pretty nice, pretty
exciting hardware technology.

00:17:29.760 --> 00:17:31.920
And we had Tim
speaking about SAP HANA

00:17:31.920 --> 00:17:34.410
and a couple of use
cases that we see

00:17:34.410 --> 00:17:36.270
how HANA can make use of that.

00:17:36.270 --> 00:17:39.000
But what does that
actually mean for SAP HANA

00:17:39.000 --> 00:17:43.200
and for SAP HANA on GCP?

00:17:43.200 --> 00:17:44.790
So this is a pretty
packed slide.

00:17:44.790 --> 00:17:46.360
I have to admit that.

00:17:46.360 --> 00:17:49.320
But it actually gives a pretty
good outline of the use cases

00:17:49.320 --> 00:17:50.970
that we see and the benefits.

00:17:50.970 --> 00:17:54.930
So basically, the
main benefit being,

00:17:54.930 --> 00:17:57.750
you can actually
process more data

00:17:57.750 --> 00:18:00.060
in real time, so at basically
the same performance

00:18:00.060 --> 00:18:02.810
as if it was in DRAM.

00:18:02.810 --> 00:18:07.343
The TCO is supposed to be
much, much more, well, better

00:18:07.343 --> 00:18:08.760
for you and for
companies, or it's

00:18:08.760 --> 00:18:12.000
cheaper than traditional DRAM.

00:18:12.000 --> 00:18:15.660
And one aspect that we
haven't really touched so far

00:18:15.660 --> 00:18:18.460
is the improved
business continuity.

00:18:18.460 --> 00:18:23.540
So the main differences between
persistent memory and DRAM

00:18:23.540 --> 00:18:25.790
is not just size and
cost, but it's also

00:18:25.790 --> 00:18:27.110
that it is persistent.

00:18:27.110 --> 00:18:31.130
So it means that it doesn't
lose the data when you shut down

00:18:31.130 --> 00:18:33.890
the server or the database,
which is something

00:18:33.890 --> 00:18:36.740
that, well, at some
point, you just have to do

00:18:36.740 --> 00:18:38.220
if you need to do maintenance.

00:18:38.220 --> 00:18:41.480
So for applying a database
upgrade, or an operating system

00:18:41.480 --> 00:18:43.374
upgrade, or just to--

00:18:43.374 --> 00:18:43.874
yeah.

00:18:46.620 --> 00:18:49.160
And so you can
actually imagine what

00:18:49.160 --> 00:18:51.660
it means for running a
memory database like HANA.

00:18:51.660 --> 00:18:55.710
So all the data is kept in
main memory at all times

00:18:55.710 --> 00:18:57.650
to be able to process
it and to process it

00:18:57.650 --> 00:19:00.980
with the speed and a performance
that you actually expect

00:19:00.980 --> 00:19:03.130
and that HANA is promising.

00:19:03.130 --> 00:19:05.730
But it also means that if
you shut down the database,

00:19:05.730 --> 00:19:09.270
all the DRAM and data gets
de-allocated, giving it back

00:19:09.270 --> 00:19:11.350
to the operating system.

00:19:11.350 --> 00:19:13.560
And when the database
is coming back up,

00:19:13.560 --> 00:19:16.740
the database SAP HANA
needs to reload that

00:19:16.740 --> 00:19:20.610
from a persistent layer, which
is the case of SAP HANA's

00:19:20.610 --> 00:19:23.400
just simple storage.

00:19:23.400 --> 00:19:25.940
Now SAP's offering
or positioning

00:19:25.940 --> 00:19:28.950
HANA in big data environments,
so for large amounts

00:19:28.950 --> 00:19:31.020
of data into terabytes scale.

00:19:31.020 --> 00:19:33.780
And if you imagine, so no matter
how fast the storage actually

00:19:33.780 --> 00:19:35.560
is, it takes some time.

00:19:35.560 --> 00:19:41.430
So if you need to load six
terabytes of data from an SSD,

00:19:41.430 --> 00:19:44.050
even if it's a very fast
SSD, into main memory,

00:19:44.050 --> 00:19:47.730
this can take a
considerable amount of time.

00:19:47.730 --> 00:19:51.810
So what we were able
to achieve in this area

00:19:51.810 --> 00:19:54.730
is an improvement
by a factor of 12.5.

00:19:54.730 --> 00:19:57.060
So it is based on
the lab example

00:19:57.060 --> 00:19:59.520
that we did before
Sapphire last year.

00:19:59.520 --> 00:20:01.920
So we were able to reduce
the startup time, including

00:20:01.920 --> 00:20:07.080
the data-loading phase from
15 minutes to just 4 minutes.

00:20:07.080 --> 00:20:09.000
So this means your
database is basically

00:20:09.000 --> 00:20:14.580
going to be back much faster
after a maintenance phase

00:20:14.580 --> 00:20:15.390
than it was before.

00:20:19.587 --> 00:20:21.170
And, of course, SAP
is also very proud

00:20:21.170 --> 00:20:23.060
on the long-standing
collaboration

00:20:23.060 --> 00:20:25.370
so that we actually
are able to announce

00:20:25.370 --> 00:20:27.860
that we are the first major
database that is fully

00:20:27.860 --> 00:20:29.720
supporting that.

00:20:29.720 --> 00:20:32.270
We touched briefly
the two operating

00:20:32.270 --> 00:20:33.710
modes of persistent
memory before,

00:20:33.710 --> 00:20:35.840
so memory mode and app direct.

00:20:35.840 --> 00:20:39.110
Memory mode is basically the--

00:20:39.110 --> 00:20:41.310
you can run every
application in memory mode.

00:20:41.310 --> 00:20:43.560
But to actually take full
advantage of the technology,

00:20:43.560 --> 00:20:45.470
including the
persistency aspects,

00:20:45.470 --> 00:20:47.900
that takes the app direct mode.

00:20:47.900 --> 00:20:49.460
And this is also--

00:20:49.460 --> 00:20:52.850
so we spent significant efforts
in our engineering department

00:20:52.850 --> 00:20:55.072
to actually enable SAP
HANA for that technology.

00:20:55.072 --> 00:20:57.530
And we got great assistance
from Intel in that, by the way.

00:21:02.080 --> 00:21:05.200
So basically, summarizing
that again, so you get

00:21:05.200 --> 00:21:10.920
reduced TCO and four
times increase in density,

00:21:10.920 --> 00:21:14.330
so from under 28
to 512 gigabyte.

00:21:14.330 --> 00:21:19.400
These are the ones that
are available right now.

00:21:19.400 --> 00:21:21.550
And you can also, you
have to put a potential

00:21:21.550 --> 00:21:23.450
to simplify your landscape.

00:21:23.450 --> 00:21:25.510
So we have seen a
couple of examples

00:21:25.510 --> 00:21:29.620
before, so because you can
make use of that capacity

00:21:29.620 --> 00:21:34.480
to put in more data, or just
install two databases on one

00:21:34.480 --> 00:21:39.140
physical server or one server in
general, so it can, of course,

00:21:39.140 --> 00:21:41.580
also be virtualized.

00:21:41.580 --> 00:21:45.070
This, in some cases, allows
you to actually avoid

00:21:45.070 --> 00:21:46.780
a scale-out scenario,
so a scenario

00:21:46.780 --> 00:21:51.970
where you logically
combine two servers to one.

00:21:51.970 --> 00:21:53.580
I mean, it's
basically two servers.

00:21:53.580 --> 00:21:56.200
So you also, in terms
of operating that,

00:21:56.200 --> 00:22:00.560
it becomes more
complex, which is also

00:22:00.560 --> 00:22:05.230
pretty good value proposition
is the aspect of data tiering.

00:22:05.230 --> 00:22:09.730
So persistent memory is not per
se a data-tiering technology.

00:22:09.730 --> 00:22:11.870
So that's not how
it is positioned,

00:22:11.870 --> 00:22:13.962
and it also would not
be fair to call it

00:22:13.962 --> 00:22:16.420
that because the performance
is actually not for warm data,

00:22:16.420 --> 00:22:18.370
so you don't have any
performance difference

00:22:18.370 --> 00:22:19.940
if you keep data there.

00:22:19.940 --> 00:22:22.870
But it certainly
has an influence

00:22:22.870 --> 00:22:25.240
on how you do data
tiering or whether you

00:22:25.240 --> 00:22:27.730
have to do data tiering
in the first place.

00:22:27.730 --> 00:22:33.460
Because let's assume you have
50% of your database size

00:22:33.460 --> 00:22:35.680
is warm data, data
that you could actually

00:22:35.680 --> 00:22:39.490
move somewhere else, by
using just DIMMS that

00:22:39.490 --> 00:22:42.760
are twice as large, you can
just keep that in your hot tier.

00:22:42.760 --> 00:22:44.860
You have the same
performance for the hot data,

00:22:44.860 --> 00:22:46.870
and what I think is
even more important,

00:22:46.870 --> 00:22:49.810
you don't need to go through
that whole project of actually

00:22:49.810 --> 00:22:52.360
identifying what
data is warm and you

00:22:52.360 --> 00:22:53.650
don't need to move the data.

00:22:53.650 --> 00:22:55.150
You can just leave it as it is.

00:22:55.150 --> 00:22:57.860
It is simpler from an
operations perspective.

00:22:57.860 --> 00:23:03.850
And you yeah, also,
basically, you

00:23:03.850 --> 00:23:06.400
don't need to add
an additional system

00:23:06.400 --> 00:23:10.090
like dynamic tiering or another
external code data tier.

00:23:13.770 --> 00:23:14.270
Yeah.

00:23:14.270 --> 00:23:18.500
I explained about the business
continuity aspect already,

00:23:18.500 --> 00:23:24.350
so it is 12.5 times improvement
in restart times or data

00:23:24.350 --> 00:23:26.480
loading times at startup.

00:23:26.480 --> 00:23:30.830
This lab example, we have
actually proved that many times

00:23:30.830 --> 00:23:32.870
during the last year.

00:23:32.870 --> 00:23:37.380
So we've done a couple of POCs
with customers, so real life

00:23:37.380 --> 00:23:39.540
customer systems,
and sometimes we've

00:23:39.540 --> 00:23:43.620
even seen much higher
factors, so up to factor 21.

00:23:43.620 --> 00:23:47.620
Depends a little bit on the
actual size of the database.

00:23:47.620 --> 00:23:50.280
So because the
larger your system

00:23:50.280 --> 00:23:53.070
is, the more time
you save basically

00:23:53.070 --> 00:23:55.148
by not having to load
that data and the higher,

00:23:55.148 --> 00:23:56.190
also, the factor becomes.

00:24:00.760 --> 00:24:03.300
So on Google Cloud.

00:24:03.300 --> 00:24:07.560
So Google Cloud has also been
a very collaborative partner

00:24:07.560 --> 00:24:12.090
for Intel and SAP
in the last year.

00:24:12.090 --> 00:24:16.320
And today, Google Cloud is
actually the first public cloud

00:24:16.320 --> 00:24:19.200
provider to offer
virtual machines that

00:24:19.200 --> 00:24:22.480
use persistent memory.

00:24:22.480 --> 00:24:26.640
So the instances that GCP
is offering, it's a 96 vCPU,

00:24:26.640 --> 00:24:30.680
7 terabyte virtual machine,
so 7 terabyte combined

00:24:30.680 --> 00:24:37.510
DRAM and persistent memory, of
which 80% is actually obtained,

00:24:37.510 --> 00:24:41.530
so persistent memory
a factor of 1 to 4.

00:24:41.530 --> 00:24:44.820
This basically brings you
a higher overall capacity

00:24:44.820 --> 00:24:48.650
at a lower cost or at the
same cost as a smaller one.

00:24:48.650 --> 00:24:50.450
Then you should
just compare that

00:24:50.450 --> 00:24:52.150
to a system with just DRAM.

00:24:57.730 --> 00:24:58.230
OK.

00:24:58.230 --> 00:25:04.137
So to put a couple more
numbers behind that promise

00:25:04.137 --> 00:25:05.720
that we make in terms
of restart time,

00:25:05.720 --> 00:25:08.180
and to also show you
how it actually works,

00:25:08.180 --> 00:25:12.090
or how HANA is designed and what
the startup time is actually

00:25:12.090 --> 00:25:14.520
composed of, so there
are two components

00:25:14.520 --> 00:25:16.360
that are pretty stable.

00:25:16.360 --> 00:25:18.450
It's like the HANA
shutdown and HANA startup.

00:25:18.450 --> 00:25:20.960
This is just to basically
load the binaries

00:25:20.960 --> 00:25:22.630
and to shut down the software.

00:25:22.630 --> 00:25:26.100
So this stays
pretty much the same

00:25:26.100 --> 00:25:28.350
no matter what
data size you have

00:25:28.350 --> 00:25:30.710
and also whether you use
DRAM or persistent memory.

00:25:30.710 --> 00:25:33.580
So there's not too much that
you can actually improve there.

00:25:33.580 --> 00:25:35.620
But what is really,
really important

00:25:35.620 --> 00:25:42.760
is the time that it takes you
until your database is back

00:25:42.760 --> 00:25:45.070
for normal operations,
so the time

00:25:45.070 --> 00:25:48.250
until you can use your
database in the same way

00:25:48.250 --> 00:25:50.890
and with the same performance as
you could before you shut down

00:25:50.890 --> 00:25:52.970
the database.

00:25:52.970 --> 00:25:54.050
And this is-- yeah.

00:25:54.050 --> 00:25:58.810
Here these bars on
the very right one,

00:25:58.810 --> 00:26:00.490
so with persistent
memory, in this case,

00:26:00.490 --> 00:26:03.720
it took 23 seconds
compared to 280,

00:26:03.720 --> 00:26:05.760
which roughly corresponds
to that factor

00:26:05.760 --> 00:26:06.880
that we've seen before.

00:26:06.880 --> 00:26:08.380
And this difference
will, of course,

00:26:08.380 --> 00:26:09.940
increase the larger
your systems get.

00:26:09.940 --> 00:26:11.070
So if you have--

00:26:11.070 --> 00:26:13.110
I mean, HANA is
available in sizes up

00:26:13.110 --> 00:26:15.510
to 24 terabytes for
a single instance,

00:26:15.510 --> 00:26:18.870
so you can expect even larger
improvements if you're actually

00:26:18.870 --> 00:26:21.020
running a system in that size.

00:26:27.480 --> 00:26:29.810
So this slide is actually
very similar from what

00:26:29.810 --> 00:26:31.940
it tries to tell.

00:26:31.940 --> 00:26:37.590
So how did downtime of a typical
SAP system actually looks like?

00:26:37.590 --> 00:26:40.330
So there is typically a phase
where you prepare your downtime

00:26:40.330 --> 00:26:43.010
in the very beginning, so
you prepare the application

00:26:43.010 --> 00:26:47.030
to actually make it ready and
to tell the [INAUDIBLE] database

00:26:47.030 --> 00:26:49.226
I'm going to be shut down.

00:26:49.226 --> 00:26:52.460
It takes a couple of minutes,
so that's actually not too long.

00:26:52.460 --> 00:26:55.070
And then there's, in
this slide, there's

00:26:55.070 --> 00:26:57.783
a pretty large
downtime of 45 minutes.

00:26:57.783 --> 00:26:59.450
So this is actually
the maintenance task

00:26:59.450 --> 00:27:04.760
that you need to execute
on your database.

00:27:04.760 --> 00:27:06.480
The duration of data,
of course, varies.

00:27:06.480 --> 00:27:10.400
So 45 minutes is already
a pretty large one,

00:27:10.400 --> 00:27:13.640
so like an operating system
upgrade or a HANA database

00:27:13.640 --> 00:27:15.820
upgrade.

00:27:15.820 --> 00:27:17.980
It's also a time that
you cannot really avoid,

00:27:17.980 --> 00:27:20.980
so this is just what
it takes to get ready.

00:27:20.980 --> 00:27:25.390
And then the interesting part
is when you start to host again.

00:27:25.390 --> 00:27:29.900
So you see on the upper
one, the upper bar,

00:27:29.900 --> 00:27:33.160
shows persistent
memory obtained,

00:27:33.160 --> 00:27:36.110
where after just four
minutes after you instantiate

00:27:36.110 --> 00:27:38.510
or basically
re-instantiate a database,

00:27:38.510 --> 00:27:42.350
it is back with the same
performance as it was before.

00:27:42.350 --> 00:27:48.130
If you compare that to how it
looks with DRAM, in this case,

00:27:48.130 --> 00:27:51.800
so the data, when it's loaded,
it's, of course, prioritized,

00:27:51.800 --> 00:27:54.300
so the most important
data is loaded first

00:27:54.300 --> 00:27:57.930
so that the system can come
back as fast as possible.

00:27:57.930 --> 00:28:02.490
So 34 minutes it takes in
this case to get to 2 and 1/2

00:28:02.490 --> 00:28:06.030
terabyte ready with the
most important tables

00:28:06.030 --> 00:28:09.840
so that your system
can go back to--

00:28:09.840 --> 00:28:13.260
actually [INAUDIBLE].

00:28:13.260 --> 00:28:15.930
But the time it takes
for the full 5 terabyte

00:28:15.930 --> 00:28:19.320
is even longer so, in
this case, almost an hour.

00:28:19.320 --> 00:28:22.310
So you can imagine what that
actually means for your system.

00:28:25.260 --> 00:28:26.850
And with this, I'd
like to hand over

00:28:26.850 --> 00:28:32.271
to Rohit who will tell a little
bit more about the roadmap.

00:28:32.271 --> 00:28:34.810
ROHIT KAMATH:
Thank you, Andreas.

00:28:34.810 --> 00:28:39.730
So here, just to give you guys
a quick overview of Google's

00:28:39.730 --> 00:28:42.940
journey on providing
system sizes,

00:28:42.940 --> 00:28:45.460
Google has been true to
providing all the systems

00:28:45.460 --> 00:28:47.290
as virtual machines so far.

00:28:47.290 --> 00:28:48.760
So you'll see on
the roadmap, when

00:28:48.760 --> 00:28:54.130
we started off back in 2017
was the first HANA system that

00:28:54.130 --> 00:28:56.320
was 208-gig or memory.

00:28:56.320 --> 00:28:57.950
And we have come a long way.

00:28:57.950 --> 00:29:01.300
Now, if you look
at Q4 2018, we're

00:29:01.300 --> 00:29:05.530
able to release up to a
4-terabyte single node

00:29:05.530 --> 00:29:07.450
machine that's available.

00:29:07.450 --> 00:29:11.590
And now, with the partnership
from SAP and Intel and Google,

00:29:11.590 --> 00:29:15.670
now we're able to release the
first 7-terabyte system that's

00:29:15.670 --> 00:29:18.400
running on Optane memory.

00:29:18.400 --> 00:29:20.050
And further down
the road map, you'll

00:29:20.050 --> 00:29:25.130
see a couple of
months down the road,

00:29:25.130 --> 00:29:28.180
we will be releasing the
6-terabyte and the 12-terabyte,

00:29:28.180 --> 00:29:30.850
again, single node
virtual machines

00:29:30.850 --> 00:29:33.640
that will be available to
run HANA as single node scale

00:29:33.640 --> 00:29:34.330
up mode.

00:29:34.330 --> 00:29:37.100
And then around the
end of the year,

00:29:37.100 --> 00:29:41.230
we will be releasing the
18-terabyte VM again.

00:29:41.230 --> 00:29:44.440
So to conclude today,
thanks to Intel and SAP

00:29:44.440 --> 00:29:49.030
to introduce you folks
to the Intel Optane DC

00:29:49.030 --> 00:29:52.360
persistent memory-based
HANA systems that will

00:29:52.360 --> 00:29:56.110
be available in Google Cloud.

00:29:56.110 --> 00:29:58.300
We have information available,
further information.

00:29:58.300 --> 00:30:02.290
You can always contact
Tim, Andreas, and Zach

00:30:02.290 --> 00:30:03.280
for further questions.

00:30:03.280 --> 00:30:06.630
